{"name":"Lagoon Rock","hex":"#43bcbe","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lagoon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shallow body of water separated from deeper sea by a bar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lagoon"},{"term":"rock","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formation of minerals, specifically:\n  The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust.","The naturally occurring aggregate of solid mineral matter that constitutes a significant part of the earth's crust."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pelt with rocks; to stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rock"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lagoon","blocks":[{"text":"A lagoon is a shallow body of water separated from a larger body of water by a narrow landform, such as a reef, a barrier island or islands, a barrier peninsula, or an isthmus. Lagoons are commonly divided into coastal lagoons and atoll lagoons. They have also been identified as occurring on mixed-sand and gravel coastlines. There is an overlap between bodies of water classified as coastal lagoons and bodies of water classified as estuaries. Lagoons are common coastal features around many parts of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/BalosLagoonCreta.jpg/330px-BalosLagoonCreta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagoon","wikidata":"Q187223"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Kara-Bogaz_Gol_from_space%2C_September_1995.jpg/500px-Kara-Bogaz_Gol_from_space%2C_September_1995.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Kara-Bogaz_Gol_from_space%2C_September_1995.jpg/500px-Kara-Bogaz_Gol_from_space%2C_September_1995.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_cam